  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Commit together",
      "items": [
        "the domain row and the outbox row commit in the same JTA transaction — the outbox row is the courier, so there is no dual-write gap to lose an event in",
        "the use case owns that transaction: @Transactional on the entry method, never on the resource or the repository; the outbox adapter joins the same EntityManager",
        "the row carries tenant_id, the event type, the payload, the idempotency key and created_at — so a drain never crosses a tenant"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Claim, publish, settle",
      "items": [
        "the worker claims the due rows with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED — a row is never handed to two workers at once, and no lease has to be tuned",
        "after the publish succeeds the row is marked sent; a failure keeps it due, backs off with jitter and, at max attempts, becomes a dead-letter row an admin can see",
        "the job is bounded — batch size, time budget, backoff, dead-letter — and runs under @Schedule or ManagedScheduledExecutorService, never a raw thread"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "At-least-once, on purpose",
      "items": [
        "JMS delivers at-least-once, so the consumer is idempotent: it deduplicates by the idempotency key, which the schema also keeps UNIQUE on outbox",
        "the message carries tenantId, correlationId and the idempotency key, so the effect can be traced from the HTTP request through the outbox row to the consumer",
        "a replay is a no-op, not a duplicate effect — the same guarantee the use case gives the caller when it replays an Idempotency-Key"
      ]
    }
  ]
